::STAT_U01_L03_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A class records the number of minutes each student spends reading in one night. Which display is appropriate for showing the distribution?
{
~A. Two-way table#A two-way table is for two categorical variables, not one numerical variable.
~B. Pie chart#A pie chart shows parts of a whole for categories, not a numerical distribution.
=C. Dot plot#A dot plot shows individual values for one numerical variable.
~D. Scatterplot#A scatterplot needs two numerical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A data set records study hours and test scores for each student. Which display fits the question about relationship?
{
=A. Scatterplot#A scatterplot shows the relationship between two numerical variables.
~B. Histogram#A histogram shows one numerical variable, not a relationship.
~C. Bar chart#A bar chart compares categories, not two numerical measurements.
~D. Pie chart#A pie chart summarizes categorical parts of a whole.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display groups one numerical variable into intervals?
{
~A. Bar chart#Bar charts compare category counts, not numerical intervals.
=B. Histogram#Histograms group numerical values into intervals to show distribution.
~C. Two-way table#Two-way tables organize categorical combinations.
~D. Scatterplot#Scatterplots show paired numerical values rather than intervals.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A coach wants a quick summary of median, quartiles, and range for player heights. Which display fits best?
{
~A. Pie chart#Pie charts show categorical parts of a whole.
~B. Bar chart#Bar charts compare category counts.
~C. Scatterplot#Scatterplots show relationships between two numerical variables.
=D. Box plot#A box plot summarizes center and spread using quartiles and extremes.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Why would a bar chart be a weak choice for raw times in seconds?
{
=A. Times are numerical values, so a distribution display such as a dot plot or histogram fits better#A bar chart is for categories; numerical times need a display that shows distribution.
~B. Times cannot be displayed in any graph#Numerical times can be displayed in dot plots, histograms, or box plots.
~C. Bar charts are only for two numerical variables#Bar charts are mainly for category counts.
~D. All numerical data require a scatterplot#Scatterplots are for two numerical variables, not every numerical dataset.
}
